---
/**
 * The method, stated plainly. It must describe what the build ACTUALLY does —
 * if the gate changes, this page changes with it.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Where do the facts on this site come from?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "From the body that sets them — the park, the county, the town — by phone or from its own published notice. Never from another guide or a review site.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What do the labels mean?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Confirmed means we called or visited. Read from the source means it came from a named primary source but has not yet been confirmed by phone. Anything whose only origin is an aggregator is not published at all.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What happens when a fact turns out to be wrong?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "We fix it, re-date it, and say on the page that it was corrected and when. Corrections are not quietly edited away.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How we verify — Deep Creek Field Guide"
  description="Every fact here carries the date it was checked and the source it came from. The method in full, including what we refuse to publish and why."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">Method</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[15ch] text-slate">How we verify</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[58ch]">
      We go to the source, we date every fact, and we publish corrections. That's
      the whole method. It is unglamorous and it is the entire reason this site
      exists.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it needs saying</h2>
    <p>
      Swallow Falls started requiring a weekend day-use reservation in May 2026.
      Months later, much of what you'll find still tells you to turn up and
      park. That's not anyone being careless — it's that nobody re-checks a page
      they wrote three years ago, and nobody wrote down where the fact came from
      in the first place.
    </p>
    <p>
      We are not exempt from that. We published the Swallow Falls rule as
      every-day, because Garrett County's announcement says every day. Maryland
      DNR runs the park, and DNR says weekends and holidays. A reader caught it.
      <a href="/parks/swallow-falls/#corrected">The correction is on the park
      page</a>, which is the only version of this promise that means anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      Getting that wrong costs a three-hour drive from Baltimore. So the useful
      thing isn't more description of the waterfall; it's knowing when the answer
      was last true.
    </p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>

    <h3>1. The source is the body that sets the rule</h3>
    <p>
      A park rule comes from the park or the agency that runs it. A town park's
      hours come from the town. A fee comes from whoever collects it. We do not
      take a fact from another guide, ever, including when that guide is probably
      right.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Every page says which kind of fact it is</h3>
    <p>
      There are two honest answers to "how do you know that", and they aren't the
      same, so they're labelled separately rather than blurred into one word.
    </p>

    <dl class="sheet breakout my-6 divide-y-2 divide-lake/25">
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label text-flag-green-bearing">Confirmed</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          We called, visited, or asked the body that sets the rule. The page
          carries a stamp with the date and the name of whoever told us.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label text-contour-deep">Read from the source</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          Taken from a named primary source — the county's own announcement,
          Maryland DNR's park page, a town's own listing — but not yet confirmed
          by phone. The page says so and names the source.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p>
      Both get published. The difference is stated, because "the county published
      this" and "we rang the county and asked" are different claims and you
      deserve to know which one you're reading. Most of this site is currently
      the second kind, and it says so on every page.
    </p>

    <h3>3. What we will not publish</h3>
    <p>
      A fact whose only origin is another guide, a review site or an aggregator
      does not go in. It gets no stamp, it's marked
      <code class="stamp">noindex</code>, and it stays out of the sitemap until
      someone has actually checked it. That's enforced by the site's build rather
      than by good intentions, so a draft can't ship as a fact by accident.
    </p>

    <h3>4. "Not confirmed" is a real answer</h3>
    <p>
      Where we don't know something, the page says so rather than guessing or
      quietly leaving it out. An empty field on a park listing means we haven't
      checked it — it does not mean the park lacks the thing. Most of the
      restroom fields on the town parks read "not confirmed" right now, and
      that's the truth rather than an oversight.
    </p>

    <h3>5. When sources conflict, the one that sets the rule wins</h3>
    <p>
      We don't average two sources and we don't split the difference. We ask
      which one actually sets the rule, publish that, and say on the page that
      the other exists and what it claims.
    </p>
    <p>
      The live example is Swallow Falls. Garrett County's May 2026 announcement
      says a reservation is required every day; Maryland DNR says weekends and
      holidays, in three separate places. DNR runs the park and takes the
      bookings, so DNR wins and the county's version is recorded on the page as
      a conflict rather than deleted. Where neither source outranks the other,
      the field stays out of the published answer entirely and goes to the top
      of the call list.
    </p>

    <h3 id="corrections">6. Corrections are published, not buried</h3>
    <p>
      When we get something wrong, we fix it, re-date it, and say on the page
      what we had said and what it actually is. The correction sits above the
      sourcing, not under it. A site that claims to be verified and silently
      rewrites its mistakes is worth less than one that never claimed it.
    </p>
    <p>
      One correction so far, on 3 August 2026:
      <a href="/parks/swallow-falls/#corrected">the Swallow Falls reservation
      days</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Found something wrong?</h2>
    <p>
      Tell us and we'll check it. Locals know these parks better than any
      publisher does, and a correction from someone who was standing there
      yesterday is worth more than another phone call.
      <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Get in touch through Werth Design</a>,
      who build and maintain this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/verified/">Every fact on the site, with its source and age</a></li>
      <li><a href="/parks/">The state parks</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
